<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/advanced/international-trade">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Trade economics rests on one of the oldest, most-verified and most-misunderstood results in
    the whole subject: <strong>comparative advantage</strong>. Countries gain by specialising
    where they are <em>relatively</em> best — not absolutely best — and exchanging; the gains are
    real, large and, since 1990, have accompanied the largest escape from poverty in recorded
    history as global trade quintupled. But the same arithmetic that creates the surplus also
    decides <em>who</em> captures it, and the losses land on identifiable towns and workers while
    the gains diffuse invisibly across millions of consumers. This unit follows the whole chain:
    why trade happens at all, what restrictions do to it, how the money crosses borders, and how
    the accounts keep score. "The gains exist" is not an argument that the gains were shared —
    that distinction is the unit's spine.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ricardo's logic, modern evidence, and the honest ledger" widget="equationStep" config={tradeCfg} />

  <h2>Specialisation and free trade: Ricardo's arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Run Ricardo's own example from the <em>Principles</em> (1817). Producing one unit of cloth
    takes England 100 labour-years and Portugal 90; one unit of wine takes England 120 and
    Portugal 80. Portugal is better at <em>both</em> — an absolute advantage in everything. The
    naive conclusion is that trade is pointless for England; the correct one is that it is
    essential. England's opportunity cost of one wine is 1.2 cloth (120/100); Portugal's is about
    0.89 cloth (80/90). Wine is <em>relatively</em> cheaper in Portugal, cloth in England, so
    specialisation creates a surplus. If each shifts its labour and they exchange one cloth for
    one wine: England buys wine at a cost of 100 labour-years instead of 120 — a 17 per cent
    saving; Portugal buys cloth at 80 instead of 90 — an 11 per cent saving. Any exchange ratio
    between 0.89 and 1.2 cloth per wine leaves both countries better off; the ratio inside that
    band (the <strong>terms of trade</strong>) decides who gains more, which is why developing
    countries have historically fought hard over it. Two centuries of evidence — from the
    nineteenth-century Atlantic grain trade to China's post-1978 export boom — keep confirming
    the mechanism: openness plus specialisation raises total output, and the gains grow with
    learning-by-doing as industries scale (South Korean steel and shipbuilding, protected and
    subsidised in the 1970s, became world leaders by the 1990s). Specialisation also reshapes
    what a country <em>is</em>: endowments that once looked like destiny — Dutch ports, Saudi
    geology, Costa Rican climate — become tradeable advantages, and the composition of exports,
    raw goods versus manufactures, is itself a development strategy.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a country only benefits from trade if it is the best at
    making something." Advantage is <em>relative</em>, by definition. The lawyer who types faster
    than her assistant still hires the assistant, because an hour in court earns ten times an
    hour of typing. Countries are the same: France and Germany export cars <em>to each other</em>
    — intra-industry trade is around half of rich-country trade — because both exploit their
    relative strengths and scale economies inside the same industry.
  </p>

  <h2>Globalisation and trade restrictions</h2>
  <p>
    Globalisation is trade plus the plumbing: capital flows, migration, and the collapse of
    transport and communication costs. The container is the underrated hero — Marc Levinson's
    <em>The Box</em> recounts that loading loose cargo cost $5.86 per ton before containers and
    roughly 16 cents after, a fall so steep that distance itself became nearly free. Against
    that current, governments erect <strong>trade restrictions</strong>: tariffs (taxes on
    imports), quotas (quantity caps), subsidies (support for local producers) and technical
    rules that quietly discriminate. The welfare accounting for a small-economy tariff: take a
    good with a world price of 100 and a tariff of 20. Domestic price rises to 120, domestic
    output expands and imports shrink; consumers lose surplus, producers gain, and the
    government collects the tariff on what is still imported. But two wedges go to nobody: the
    <em>production distortion</em> (domestic firms make units at above world cost) and the
    <em>consumption distortion</em> (buyers priced out of units they value above world cost) —
    with plausible numbers, say 10 extra home units and 10 lost purchases, each wedge costs
    about ½ × 10 × 20 = 100, so 200 of society's surplus is destroyed outright while the rest
    is redistributed. Tariffs protect a <em>job</em> by taxing every <em>consumer</em>; studies
    of the 2018 US steel tariffs found each job saved cost consumers hundreds of thousands of
    dollars a year. And restrictions invite retaliation: after the Smoot–Hawley tariff of 1930,
    world trade fell by roughly two-thirds by 1934, deepening the Depression for everyone —
    the coordinate logic of a prisoner's dilemma played between nations. The WTO exists precisely
    to keep that dilemma solved: most-favoured-nation treatment, bound tariffs and binding
    dispute settlement. The restrictions also differ in who pays: a tariff raises revenue for
    the government and lets the market ration the quantity, while a quota freezes the quantity
    and hands the scarcity premium to whoever holds the import licence — one reason trade
    agreements bind tariffs but generally prohibit quantitative restrictions, with carve-outs
    for agriculture and temporary safeguards.
  </p>

  <h2>Foreign exchange rates: the price of money</h2>
  <p>
    Every trade crosses a currency border, and the exchange rate is the relative price that
    reconciles them. If one pound buys 1.25 dollars and the pound slides to 1.15, the pound has
    depreciated about 8 per cent (1 − 1.15/1.25). Trace the arithmetic: a 25,000-pound car
    exported to the United States cost the buyer $31,250 before and $28,750 after — the exporter
    can hold its price in pounds, cut the dollar price, or split the difference between volume
    and margin. Imports move the other way: a $100 million oil invoice costs £80 million before
    and £87 million after, which is why a depreciating currency imports inflation — a
    disproportionately painful shock for countries that import food and fuel. Rates move on
    trade flows, interest differentials (the <em>carry trade</em>: borrowing in cheap currencies
    to invest in dear ones), inflation expectations and sheer speculation; the 1992 "Black
    Wednesday" crisis, when speculators led by George Soros attacked the pound's fixed ERM
    parity, forced UK interest rates from 10 toward 15 per cent in a single day before Britain
    left the system — proof that a fixed rate is a promise whose credibility, not just its
    arithmetic, can be tested by markets. Over long horizons, <strong>purchasing power
    parity</strong> anchors the exchange rate to what money actually buys: a basket that costs
    100 in one country and 50 in another hints the second currency is undervalued, which is why
    economists compare GDP across countries at PPP, not market rates — a haircut costing a tenth
    as much in Nairobi as in New York means nominal comparisons understate real Kenyan income.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a devaluation is a free competitiveness boost." It works only
    if buyers abroad actually respond to the new prices — the Marshall–Lerner condition — and in
    the short run they usually cannot: contracts are fixed, orders are placed, so volumes adjust
    slowly while the import bill for fuel, food and parts rises immediately. The trade balance
    typically <em>worsens</em> for a year or more before improving — the J-curve — and the whole
    time, depreciation is quietly a transfer from every consumer's real wage toward exporters.
    It is policy with a distribution, not a spell; that is why competitive devaluations, tried
    by several countries at once, just export inflation to each other.
  </p>

  <h2>The current account: the country's scorecard</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>current account</strong> is the comprehensive record of a country's transactions
    with the world: the balance of trade in goods and in services, plus <em>primary income</em>
    (interest, profits and dividends earned across borders) and <em>secondary income</em>
    (remittances and aid). Worked example: a country exports 500 billion, imports 600, pays 30
    more in profit and interest to foreign investors than it receives, and receives 50 billion
    in remittances from workers abroad — its current account is 500 − 600 − 30 + 50 = −80
    billion, a deficit of 80. A deficit is not a loss; it is arithmetic that must be financed
    by a capital inflow — foreigners acquiring the country's bonds, firms or property — which
    is why "current account + capital account (plus statistical error) = 0" is an identity, not
    a policy choice. Deficits can be benign (a developing country importing capital goods to
    build its future earning power, the way South Korea ran deficits through its boom years) or
    dangerous (financed by short-term dollar debt that flees at the first wobble — the classic
    prelude to crises in Asia 1997 and Argentina repeatedly). Scale matters: a deficit near
    3 per cent of GDP, roughly where the United States has sat for years, is financeable by the
    world's reserve-currency issuer; a deficit above 8–10 per cent has historically been a
    red flag. Persistent surpluses carry their own costs — Germany's, above 6 per cent of GDP
    for most of the 2010s, drew criticism for storing German saving in foreign assets while
    under-investing at home.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a current account deficit means the country is losing money
    or going broke." A deficit means the country is investing or consuming more than it
    produces, financed by foreign capital — exactly what the young United States did throughout
    the nineteenth century and Australia has done for most of its history. The right questions
    are what the borrowed resources build and how durable the financing is, not the sign of the
    number itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The elasticity fine print.</strong> A devaluation only improves the trade balance if
    the <strong>Marshall–Lerner condition</strong> holds — export and import demand elasticities
    must sum to more than one — and in the short run it usually does not: contracts are fixed,
    volumes adjust slowly, so the balance first worsens and traces the famous <strong>J-curve</strong>
    before improving. Britain after its 1967 devaluation and the US after 1985 both traced it.
    The <strong>Prebisch–Singer hypothesis</strong> pushes the terms-of-trade point historically:
    commodity exporters face raw-material prices that trend down against manufactures, an
    argument that shaped a half-century of import-substitution policy in Latin America.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>New trade theory and the value-added puzzle.</strong> Paul Krugman (Nobel 2008)
    showed that scale economies and network effects make some trade flow between similar
    countries in similar goods, driven by firms' fixed costs rather than endowments — and that
    strategic protection can, in narrow models, raise welfare, which is why the infant-industry
    debate never quite dies. Global value chains then scrambled the accounting: Xing and Detert's
    famous iPhone study found that of a roughly $179 factory cost, only about $6.50 was value
    added in China — yet trade statistics booked the entire assembly value as a Chinese export.
    Trade balances computed on value added look very different, and much modern trade politics
    is aimed at a statistic that no longer measures what it seems to measure.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier.</strong> Economists now treat trade policy as a distribution problem:
    Autor, Dorn and Hanson's "China shock" research traced US manufacturing job losses into
    specific commuting zones that stayed depressed for over a decade — evidence that changed the
    profession's tone on compensation and fed directly into the industrial-policy revival
    (chips, batteries, subsidies) of the 2020s. Weaponised interdependence is the newest chapter:
    export controls on advanced chips and the 2022 energy crisis showed that trade patterns are
    also strategic positions, and that a supply chain can be a weapon without a shot being
    fired. Ricardo's arithmetic survives intact; the governance questions built around it have
    never been more open.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ricardo, Principles (1817); Autor, Dorn & Hanson (2013); Krugman (Nobel 2008) on new trade theory.</p>
